<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.14.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00638</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.14.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>046ba798-129c-4c3f-a226-b1fe70c842dd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">In questions 1 - 5, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the system.</para>
        <exercise id="_74a88f37-794f-47ca-8826-0b457de51fd7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00005">  a. (3,−3)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00006">Correct!  (3, -3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00007">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is Yes. (3, -3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00009">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00011">The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (3, -3) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2abfa603-4fb7-439d-8aa4-313eef64088d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">b. (7,1)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00013">Correct! (7, 1) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00015">The correct answer is No. (7, 1) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>22</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>13</m:mn><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>❌</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00018">The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (7,1) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_9cacddc8-a918-46c9-99e6-6dfba810ca9f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00021">  a. (5,−2)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00022">Correct! (5, -2) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00023">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">The correct answer is No. (5, -2) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>22</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>❌</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>❌</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00027"> Since (5,-2) is not true for either system, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_458317ef-7fa0-4860-a797-a2e026c3e5c1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00028">b. (−1,3)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00029">Correct! (-1, 3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00030">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00031">The correct answer is Yes. (-1, 3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00032">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00034">The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (-1,3) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5d5f3bfa-28bc-4f5d-950e-44488d9c37b9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00037">  a. (6, −4)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00038">Correct! (6, -4) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00039">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00040">The correct answer is No. (6, -4) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00041">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>❌</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00043">The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (6,-4) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c3c6b95d-4630-45b9-a90e-ddaec70fec49" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00044">b. (3, 0)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00045">Correct! (3, 0) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00046">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00047">The correct answer is Yes. (3, 0) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00048">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00050">The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (3,0) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ed421c51-b59b-4923-a8cf-4508e29ddb1d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00053">  a. (−4,−1)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00054">Correct! (-4, -1) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00055">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00056">The correct answer is No. (-4, -1) is not a solution to the system.

    </para>
            <para id="para-00057">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>❌</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00059">The point must be true for both inequalities to be a solution to the system. Since (-4,-1) is not true for both systems, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_140ad5b7-cb3c-41a9-902d-3b47522c825a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00060">b. (8, 3)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00061">Correct!  (8, 3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00062">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00063">The correct answer is Yes. (8, 3) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00064">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00066">The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (8,3) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_62942071-43a9-444f-acdd-aa759746db45" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00069">  a. (1, −3)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00070">Correct! (1, -3) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00071">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00072">The correct answer is No. (1, -3) is not a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00073">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>21</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mi>❌</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>23</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>❌</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00075"> Since (1,-3) is not true for either system, it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e63d490a-6d71-4e3f-84c8-8619dc17c9e7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00076">b. (−4, 4)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00077">Correct! (-4, 4) is a solution to the system.</para>
            <para id="para-00078">Try again. Revisit activity 2.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00079">The correct answer is Yes. (-4, 4) is a solution to the system.
    </para>
            <para id="para-00080">When you substitute the values into the inequalities the results are:</para>
            <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mtable><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mtd></m:mtr><m:mtr><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>44</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd><m:mtd columnalign="center"/><m:mtd columnalign="right"><m:mn>36</m:mn><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>✓</m:mi></m:mtd></m:mtr></m:mtable></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00082">The point is true for both inequalities, so the ordered pair (-4,4) is a solution to the system.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_152c9fb4-de7f-403b-8f8e-a2bee36f959d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <para id="para-00083">Two inequalities are graphed on the same coordinate plane. Which region represents the solution to the system of the two inequalities?</para>
            <para id="para-00084">
              
                <media alt="Two intersecting inequalities.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_16_13_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00085">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00086">Region A</para>
            <para id="para-00087">Region B</para>
            <para id="para-00088">Region C</para>
            <para id="para-00089">Region D</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00090">Correct! Region D</para>
            <para id="para-00091">Try again. Revisit activities 2.14.3 and 2.14.4.</para>
            <para id="para-00092">The correct answer is Region D. 
        Region D represents the overlap of the graph of the inequality represented by the area shaded blue and the graph of the inequality represented by the area that is striped. 
        Region D represents the overlap of the graph of the inequality represented by the area shaded blue and the graph of the inequality represented by the area that is striped.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
